"The <strong>Pull on Earth</strong> view shows the Moon’s pull at points around Earth, or the tidal pull: the pull at each point minus the pull at Earth’s center. Drag the Moon, or use its distance slider, to move it from {#} to {#} Earth radii; today it is {#} Earth radii ({#} {#} km).",
"The <strong>Ocean bulges</strong> view shows an ocean covering the whole Earth, a city on the equator, and the water level there over two days and over a month. The readouts give the height of the Moon’s tide and of the Sun’s tide, the city’s water level, the day’s range from high to low water, and when the next high tide comes. The time controls set the city’s clock and the days since new moon.",
"Record reading works in the Pull on Earth view. It stores the Moon’s distance and the pulls in μm/s² (millionths of a meter per second squared). Export to worksheet sends the readings to the first Data table below. Read the water levels in the Ocean bulges view from the readouts.",
"The ocean in the demo follows the tidal pull at once. Real oceans are shaped by continents and basins and lag behind the Moon, so real tides range from a few centimeters to more than {#} m.",
"Find out why the ocean rises and falls twice a day, why the Moon raises bigger tides than the Sun even though the Sun pulls on Earth far harder, and why some weeks have bigger tides than others.",
"<strong>The Moon pulls the near side harder.</strong> Gravity weakens with distance. The Moon pulls hardest on the side of Earth facing it, less on Earth’s center, and least on the far side. What stretches the ocean is not the pull itself but these differences: compared with Earth’s center, the near side is pulled toward the Moon and the far side is left behind, away from it. This difference is called the <strong>tidal pull</strong>. The ocean bulges out on both sides, and Earth turns under the two bulges.",
"<strong>The tidal pull falls off faster.</strong> The Moon’s pull goes as one over the distance squared: twice as far, one quarter of the pull. The tidal pull goes as one over the distance cubed: twice as far, one eighth.",
"<strong>The Sun raises tides too.</strong> The Sun is {#} million times heavier than the Moon but {#} times farther away, and the cube of {#} is {#} million. At new moon and full moon the Sun’s and Moon’s bulges line up and add: <strong>spring tides</strong>, the biggest of the month. At first and third quarter they are at right angles and partly cancel: <strong>neap tides</strong>.",
